{{Japanese name|Miyazaki}}

'''Juichi Miyazaki''' (宮崎 寿一, ''Miyazaki Juichi''; 16 October 1831 – 12 October 1902) was a Sanese statesman, politician, and the first [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]]. A samurai of the '''Sasedo Domain''', he was a central figure in the [[Saito Restoration]] which overthrew the [[Honda Shogunate]] and restored Imperial rule. As a leading member of the '''Saito Six'''—the coalition of reformist samurai and court nobles who dominated the early restoration government—Miyazaki is widely regarded as the principal architect of the modern Sanese state.

Often called the "Father of the Constitution" ({{nihongo|憲法の父|Kenpō no Chichi}}), Miyazaki chaired the bureau which drafted the '''1889 Saito Constitution''', the first modern constitution in Fosia. Looking to the [[Volisania|Volisanian]] constitutional model for inspiration, he established a political system that balanced a powerful monarchy with a bicameral legislature, the [[Diet of Okaiken]].

Miyazaki served as Prime Minister on four separate occasions (1872–1875, 1890–1892, 1896–1898, 1900–1902). His career spanned the entirety of Okaiken's rapid modernization, from the abolition of the feudal ''han'' system to the nation's emergence as a regional power following the [[Fenian-Sanese War]]. In his final years, frustrated by parliamentary obstructionism, he broke with the tradition of "transcendent" non-partisan governance to found the '''[[Rikken Seiyūkai]]''' (Constitutional Association of Political Friends), the first modern political party in Okaiken history.

In October 1902, while traveling to [[Fenia]] to negotiate spheres of influence regarding the Kwangju peninsula, Miyazaki was assassinated at Harbin Station by '''An Joon-ho''', a [[Kwangju]] independence activist. His death marked the symbolic end of the restoration era and accelerated tensions that would eventually lead to the [[Fenian-Sanese War]] (1904–1905).

==Early life and education==
Miyazaki was born '''Hayashi Risuke''' in 1831 in the castle town of '''Sasedo''' (modern-day [[Miyagi Prefecture]]), the son of a low-ranking samurai of the Sasedo clan. His family was of modest means, but deeply loyal to the anti-Shogunate faction that dominated the domain. At age 12, he was adopted into the Miyazaki family, a slightly higher-ranking samurai household, and took the name Juichi.

He studied at the '''Sasedo Academy''' (''Shokasonjuku'') under the radical scholar '''Yoshida Shoin''', who advocated for the ''Sonnō Jōi'' ("Revere the Emperor, Expel the Barbarians") movement. Yoshida's teachings instilled in Miyazaki a fierce nationalism and a belief that the Honda Shogunate had lost its mandate to rule by failing to protect the country from foreign encroachment—particularly the [[Volisania|Volisanian]] naval demonstrations that had exposed Okaiken's military weakness.

In the 1850s, Miyazaki became involved in the radical ''shishi'' movement. He participated in several attacks on foreign legations, including protests against Volisanian commercial presence in Okami. However, realizing the futility of fighting Western technology with traditional methods, his views began to shift toward modernization.

==The "Sasedo Five" and Study in Volisania==
In 1863, defying the Shogunate's ban on foreign travel—a crime punishable by death—Miyazaki was one of five young samurai from Sasedo chosen to smuggle themselves out of the country to study in [[Volisania]]. Known as the '''Sasedo Five''', they boarded a Volisanian merchant vessel disguised as sailors.

Arriving in the Volisanian capital, the group studied at University College Volisania under the mentorship of Professor Alexander Williamson. Miyazaki focused his studies on Western gunnery, logistics, and parliamentary systems. The experience was transformative; witnessing the industrial might of the Volisanian Empire convinced him that ''Jōi'' ("Expel the Barbarians") was impossible without first adopting "Barbarian" methods. He became a lifelong admirer of Volisania and a proponent of rapid industrialization.
He returned to Okaiken in 1864, just as the Shogunate was launching a punitive expedition against the rebellious Sasedo domain. Miyazaki played a key role in negotiating a ceasefire, using his newfound knowledge of international law to prevent the total destruction of his home province.

==The Saito Restoration (1868–1871)==
===Overthrowing the Shogun===
Following his return, Miyazaki rose rapidly through the ranks of the Sasedo leadership. He became a key strategist in the anti-Shogunate alliance, brokering the critical '''Sasedo-Kagami Alliance''' in 1866. This alliance combined Sasedo's political maneuvering with Kagami's military might.

During the final campaigns against the Honda Shogunate, Miyazaki did not command troops on the front lines but served as the primary logistical coordinator for the Imperial forces. He secured modern rifles and artillery from Volisanian arms dealers, giving the Imperial army a decisive advantage over the Shogunate's samurai levies.

===The New Government===
On January 3, 1871, the young Emperor Saito issued the Decree of Restoration, dissolving the shogunate and establishing the Imperial Government of Okaiken. Miyazaki was appointed as a Junior Councilor (''San'yo'') and became one of the '''Saito Six'''—the coalition of reformist samurai and court nobles who would dominate the early restoration government.

His first major political battle was the '''Abolition of the Han System''' in 1872. Facing down armed resistance from disgruntled samurai, Miyazaki successfully replaced the feudal domains with a centralized prefecture system administered by governors appointed by Okami. This act effectively destroyed the feudal class structure and centralized all tax revenue in the hands of the Imperial government.

==First Premiership (1872–1875)==
On January 22, 1872, following a reorganization of the Council of State (''Dajō-kan''), Juichi Miyazaki was appointed the first '''[[Prime Minister of Okaiken]]''' (''Naikaku Sōri-Daijin''). At 41, he was the youngest leader in the restoration government.

===Rapid Modernization===
Miyazaki's first term was defined by the slogan ''Fukoku Kyōhei'' ("Enrich the Country, Strengthen the Army"). His government enacted a dizzying array of reforms in line with the Five Articles of the Charter Oath (April 6, 1871):
* '''Abolition of the Class System (1872):''' Ended the samurai's privileged status and established universal conscription.
* '''Land Tax Reform (1873):''' Established a unified monetary tax on land, stabilizing the government's revenue stream.
* '''Compulsory Education (1873):''' Created a national school system mandating primary schooling for all children.
* '''Bank of Okaiken (1875):''' Adopted the Sanese En as a modern currency.
* '''First Rail Line (1876):''' Connected Okami to [[Tokuyama]], beginning the nation's railway network.

===The Crisis of 1873===
In 1873, a faction of the government led by the charismatic general '''Saigō Takamori''' advocated for an immediate invasion of [[Kwangju]] to employ the idle samurai class. Miyazaki, who was part of the "Internal Construction" faction, vehemently opposed this, arguing that Okaiken was too weak to fight a foreign war and must focus on industrialization.

The dispute tore the government apart. Miyazaki ultimately prevailed by securing the support of the Emperor, forcing Saigō and his followers to resign. This victory consolidated Miyazaki's power but led directly to the [[Okawa Rebellion]] in 1877, which Miyazaki's government ruthlessly suppressed.

==Drafting the Constitution==
Following his resignation in 1875 due to exhaustion, Miyazaki turned his attention to the structure of the state. He was appointed Minister of the Imperial Household and tasked with drafting a constitution.

In 1882, he led a constitutional study mission to [[Volisania]] and [[Etrusca]]. He spent nearly 18 months studying under legal scholars in the Volisanian tradition. Miyazaki rejected the more liberal models, believing they gave too much power to the masses. Instead, he favored a constitutional framework that centralized sovereignty in the monarch while allowing for a controlled parliament.
He returned to Okami and established a Bureau for the Investigation of Constitutional Systems. Working in secrecy, he drafted the '''1889 Saito Constitution'''. The document established the Emperor as "sacred and inviolable" and created the [[Diet of Okaiken]], consisting of an appointed [[House of Officers]] and an elected [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]].

==Return to Power (1890–1900)==
===Second Premiership (1890–1892)===
Miyazaki returned to the premiership in 1890 to preside over the first session of the Imperial Diet. He expected the Diet to be a rubber stamp, but instead faced fierce resistance from the newly elected "popular rights" parties in the House of Commoners, who blocked his budget proposals. Miyazaki responded by dissolving the House and using police interference to swing the subsequent election, establishing a pattern of adversarial politics that would plague the early Diet.

===The Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding===
Although [[Mitsunari Taku]] was Prime Minister during the negotiations, Miyazaki served as the chief diplomat and elder statesman during the pivotal Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding (April 1895). This landmark agreement established Okaiken as a partner in the broader Fosian coalition alongside the [[Kingdom of Zong]], [[Nakamizu]], and [[Yanbaru]], transforming Okaiken's geopolitical position from competitor to Fosian partner.
The treaty recognized that Fosian powers shared common interests in resisting Illypnian colonial encroachment and established frameworks for joint naval operations and economic integration.

===Third Premiership (1896–1898)===
His third term was short and troubled. Faced with a unified opposition in the Diet that refused to pass tax increases for military expansion, Miyazaki resigned in frustration, paving the way for the first ever party-led cabinet under [[Ryuichi Koyanagi]].

==Founding the Seiyūkai and Fourth Premiership==
By 1900, Miyazaki realized that the "transcendent" style of government—where cabinet ministers stood above political parties—was no longer sustainable. To the shock of his fellow oligarchs (''genrō''), he descended into the political fray.
In September 1900, he founded the '''[[Rikken Seiyūkai]]''' (Constitutional Association of Political Friends). By leveraging his prestige and connections within the bureaucracy, he attracted a majority of Diet members to his new party. He became Prime Minister for the fourth time in October 1900, leading the first cabinet explicitly backed by a parliamentary majority. However, internal friction with the House of Officers and conservative bureaucrats forced his resignation in May 1901.

==Resident-General of Kwangju and Assassination==
Following his resignation, Miyazaki was appointed the first Resident-General of [[Kwangju]] in 1901. In this role, he forced the Kwangju government to sign a protectorate treaty, effectively ending its diplomatic independence.
In October 1902, Miyazaki traveled to '''Harbin''', [[Fenia]], to negotiate with Fenian officials regarding spheres of influence in Northeast Fosia. Tensions between Okaiken and the Fenian Empire had been escalating, particularly regarding the strategic Ameka Islands at the entrance to the Sea of Mitoyagi.

On the morning of October 12, 1902, as Miyazaki inspected the Fenian honor guard at Harbin Station, a Kwangju nationalist named '''An Joon-ho''' stepped out of the crowd. An fired three shots, hitting Miyazaki in the chest. Miyazaki reportedly shouted, "I have been hit!" before collapsing. He died 20 minutes later.

==Legacy==
Juichi Miyazaki is a colossal figure in Sanese history. He is credited with transforming a feudal, isolationist island nation into a modern industrial state capable of competing with established powers.
* '''Political:''' He established the institutions of the modern Sanese state: the Cabinet, the Diet, the Privy Council, and the 1889 Saito Constitution. His founding of the Seiyūkai established the mechanism for party politics that would characterize the [[Go-Shōkō Democracy]] era (1912–1930).
* '''Diplomatic:''' His role in the Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding (1895) secured Okaiken's position within the Fosian coalition that would dominate regional politics for decades.
* '''Controversy:''' While revered as a state-builder in Okaiken, he is reviled in [[Kwangju]] as the architect of colonization. His assassination is viewed in Okaiken as a national tragedy, but in Kwangju as a patriotic act of resistance.

His assassination accelerated tensions with [[Fenia]], contributing to the outbreak of the [[Fenian-Sanese War]] (1904–1905), which concluded with the Treaty of Yomitan and established Okaiken's dominance over the Ameka Islands.
His portrait was featured on the 1,000 En banknote from 1963 to 1984.

